1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,560
January fifteenth, 1919.

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:05,090
Boston's North End.

3
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:08,960
It's just after noon, and the city is
enjoying something rare...

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,810
a warm day, in the middle of winter.

5
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:14,930
Above Commercial Street stands a steel
tank.

6
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:17,920
Fifty feet tall. Ninety feet wide.

7
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:22,000
Inside it: more than two million gallons
of molasses.

8
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:24,450
And the tank has been leaking for years.

9
00:00:24,680 --> 00:00:27,240
So badly, that the company painted it
brown...

10
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:28,530
to hide the streaks.

11
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:32,290
At around twelve thirty, the steel splits
apart.

12
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:37,600
A wave of molasses, by some estimates
twenty-five feet high, rolls through the

13
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:40,450
streets at an estimated thirty-five miles
an hour.

14
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:41,920
It flattens buildings.

15
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:44,450
It bends the steel of the elevated
railway.

16
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:47,480
It catches people, horses, wagons...

17
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:49,010
and it doesn't let go.

18
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:51,280
Twenty-one people will die.

19
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:53,410
About a hundred and fifty will be injured.

20
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:55,250
And the company that owned the tank?

21
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:57,170
It will blame anarchists.

22
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:00,360
This is the story of the Great Molasses
Flood...

23
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:03,250
and the warnings that nobody wanted to
hear.

24
00:01:10,371 --> 00:01:15,131
To understand how this happened, we need
to go back four years, to the edge of

25
00:01:15,171 --> 00:01:20,491
Boston Harbor. In 1915, the Purity
Distilling Company built a storage tank at

26
00:01:20,531 --> 00:01:23,611
529 Commercial Street, in the North End.

27
00:01:23,651 --> 00:01:25,181
The location made sense.

28
00:01:25,331 --> 00:01:28,811
Ships could tie up right next to it,
unload their molasses, and pump it

29
00:01:28,851 --> 00:01:30,141
straight into the tank.

30
00:01:30,451 --> 00:01:34,411
From there, the molasses was sent by pipe
to the company's plant in Cambridge,

31
00:01:34,451 --> 00:01:37,371
across the river. Why so much molasses?

32
00:01:37,411 --> 00:01:40,221
Because molasses can be fermented into
ethanol.

33
00:01:40,371 --> 00:01:42,731
Ethanol goes into alcoholic drinks.

34
00:01:42,771 --> 00:01:45,981
But it is also a key ingredient in
munitions.

35
00:01:46,131 --> 00:01:50,541
And in the years of the First World War,
munitions were very good business.

36
00:01:50,851 --> 00:01:56,171
In 1917, Purity was bought by a much
bigger company: United States Industrial

37
00:01:56,211 --> 00:02:01,531
Alcohol, or USIA. The tank on Commercial
Street was now part of a national

38
00:02:01,571 --> 00:02:06,141
operation. And the man responsible for it
was not an engineer.

39
00:02:06,211 --> 00:02:07,261
Picture the street.

40
00:02:07,411 --> 00:02:09,931
On one side, the harbor and the piers.

41
00:02:09,971 --> 00:02:14,571
Overhead, the steel frame of the elevated
railway on Atlantic Avenue, with trains

42
00:02:14,611 --> 00:02:20,651
rattling past. Around it, warehouses, a
firehouse, homes, horses and wagons,

43
00:02:20,691 --> 00:02:22,701
and people going about their day.

44
00:02:22,771 --> 00:02:26,701
And in the middle of it all, a fifty-foot
wall of steel.

45
00:02:30,213 --> 00:02:32,143
His name was Arthur Jell.

46
00:02:32,293 --> 00:02:33,903
He was the company's treasurer.

47
00:02:34,213 --> 00:02:36,573
Jell oversaw the construction of the tank.

48
00:02:36,613 --> 00:02:39,663
He had no background in architecture or
engineering.

49
00:02:39,813 --> 00:02:43,693
He was a financial man, placed in charge
of a construction project that would hold

50
00:02:43,733 --> 00:02:45,583
millions of gallons of liquid.

51
00:02:45,813 --> 00:02:49,693
Investigators would later describe the
tank as poorly built and not tested

52
00:02:49,733 --> 00:02:54,093
enough. Before a tank like this is filled
with something heavy, the normal step is

53
00:02:54,133 --> 00:02:57,343
to test it. You fill it with water, and
you watch for leaks.

54
00:02:57,653 --> 00:02:59,983
That test was never properly done.

55
00:03:00,213 --> 00:03:05,413
The tank may have started leaking the very
first time it was filled, in 1915.

56
00:03:05,453 --> 00:03:07,253
And it kept leaking.

57
00:03:07,293 --> 00:03:12,373
Molasses seeped through the seams and ran
down the sides in long, dark streaks.

58
00:03:12,413 --> 00:03:14,173
People in the neighborhood noticed.

59
00:03:14,213 --> 00:03:17,373
Some of them brought containers and
collected the leaking molasses to take

60
00:03:17,413 --> 00:03:20,773
home. The company's answer was not to fix
the tank.

61
00:03:20,813 --> 00:03:22,573
It was to paint it brown.

62
00:03:22,613 --> 00:03:24,133
Brown, like molasses.

63
00:03:24,173 --> 00:03:26,173
So the streaks would be harder to see.

64
00:03:26,213 --> 00:03:27,933
There were other warnings, too.

65
00:03:27,973 --> 00:03:29,653
The tank made noises.

66
00:03:29,693 --> 00:03:33,293
Groaning, rumbling sounds, as the steel
took the load.

67
00:03:33,333 --> 00:03:35,373
Those warnings were ignored.

68
00:03:35,413 --> 00:03:37,053
Jell heard about the noises.

69
00:03:37,093 --> 00:03:38,933
The tank stayed in service.

70
00:03:38,973 --> 00:03:41,493
And there was pressure to keep the tank
full.

71
00:03:41,533 --> 00:03:43,093
Prohibition was coming.

72
00:03:43,133 --> 00:03:47,213
The amendment that would ban alcohol
across the country was ratified on January

73
00:03:47,253 --> 00:03:51,453
sixteenth, 1919, one day after the
disaster.

74
00:03:51,493 --> 00:03:55,533
Several historians argue that the company
was rushing to produce as much as it could

75
00:03:55,573 --> 00:03:57,023
before the law took effect.

76
00:03:57,253 --> 00:03:59,583
The ban itself would begin one year later.

77
00:03:59,733 --> 00:04:01,933
But the clock was already running.

78
00:04:01,973 --> 00:04:06,213
In its whole life, the tank was filled to
capacity only eight times.

79
00:04:06,253 --> 00:04:12,063
And on January fifteenth, 1919, it was
holding more than two million gallons.

80
00:04:15,578 --> 00:04:18,178
That winter had been bitterly cold.

81
00:04:18,218 --> 00:04:21,618
In the days before the disaster, the
temperature had been around two degrees

82
00:04:21,658 --> 00:04:24,658
Fahrenheit. Then the weather turned.

83
00:04:24,698 --> 00:04:28,018
By January fifteenth, it was above forty
degrees.

84
00:04:28,058 --> 00:04:31,738
After weeks of freezing weather, it felt
almost like spring.

85
00:04:31,778 --> 00:04:35,298
The day before, a ship had delivered a
fresh load of molasses.

86
00:04:35,338 --> 00:04:38,578
That molasses had been warmed, so it would
flow more easily.

87
00:04:38,618 --> 00:04:43,498
Warm molasses, poured into a tank full of
colder molasses, on a day when the air was

88
00:04:43,538 --> 00:04:48,258
suddenly mild. Some researchers believe
the expansion inside the tank added to the

89
00:04:48,298 --> 00:04:49,578
strain on the steel.

90
00:04:49,618 --> 00:04:51,898
Liquids take up more space when they warm.

91
00:04:51,938 --> 00:04:56,898
Not much more. But in a tank holding more
than two million gallons, a little is a

92
00:04:56,938 --> 00:05:01,938
lot. And this was a tank that was already
leaking, already groaning, built from

93
00:05:01,978 --> 00:05:05,508
steel that, as we'll see, was never strong
enough.

94
00:05:09,010 --> 00:05:12,810
At about twelve thirty, people on
Commercial Street heard a sound they would

95
00:05:12,850 --> 00:05:17,770
never forget. Some said it was a deep
roar, like an elevated train passing

96
00:05:17,810 --> 00:05:20,450
overhead. Others heard a crash.

97
00:05:20,490 --> 00:05:23,330
A growl. A bang like thunder.

98
00:05:23,370 --> 00:05:26,210
And a rapid popping, like a machine gun.

99
00:05:26,250 --> 00:05:29,130
That was the rivets, shooting out of the
steel.

100
00:05:29,170 --> 00:05:33,690
The tank split open, and more than two
million gallons of molasses came out at

101
00:05:33,730 --> 00:05:37,770
once. The wave reached about twenty-five
feet at its peak.

102
00:05:37,810 --> 00:05:39,610
Some accounts say fifteen feet.

103
00:05:39,650 --> 00:05:41,770
The memorial plaque says forty.

104
00:05:41,810 --> 00:05:44,810
It moved at around thirty-five miles an
hour.

105
00:05:44,850 --> 00:05:48,570
Pieces of the tank's steel were thrown
into the structure of the elevated railway

106
00:05:48,610 --> 00:05:49,890
on Atlantic Avenue.

107
00:05:49,930 --> 00:05:52,770
A streetcar was briefly knocked off its
tracks.

108
00:05:52,810 --> 00:05:55,130
Buildings were pushed off their
foundations.

109
00:05:55,170 --> 00:05:57,530
A truck was hurled into the harbor.

110
00:05:57,570 --> 00:06:00,810
Several blocks were flooded two to three
feet deep.

111
00:06:00,850 --> 00:06:04,220
A boy named Anthony di Stasio was walking
home from school.

112
00:06:04,370 --> 00:06:08,490
The wave picked him up and carried him on
its crest, then rolled him under as it

113
00:06:08,530 --> 00:06:10,810
slowed. Anthony survived.

114
00:06:10,850 --> 00:06:13,660
And this is what made the molasses so
deadly.

115
00:06:13,810 --> 00:06:17,370
As it spread and cooled in the winter air,
it thickened.

116
00:06:17,410 --> 00:06:21,210
People who were caught in it were trapped,
unable to pull themselves free.

117
00:06:21,250 --> 00:06:24,090
Think about what molasses is like on a
cold morning.

118
00:06:24,130 --> 00:06:27,130
Thick. Slow. Almost solid.

119
00:06:27,170 --> 00:06:31,930
On that mild afternoon, the molasses came
out of the tank warm and fast.

120
00:06:31,970 --> 00:06:34,357
But the air was still January air.

121
00:06:34,397 --> 00:06:38,010
And as the wave spread out and lost its
speed, it cooled.

122
00:06:38,050 --> 00:06:40,730
The cooler it got, the thicker it became.

123
00:06:40,770 --> 00:06:45,500
Within minutes, a fast-moving wave turned
into something closer to wet cement.

124
00:06:49,019 --> 00:06:51,219
The first help came from the water.

125
00:06:51,259 --> 00:06:55,419
A training ship of the Massachusetts
Nautical School, the USS Nantucket, was

126
00:06:55,459 --> 00:07:00,019
docked nearby. One hundred and sixteen
cadets, led by Lieutenant Commander H.

127
00:07:00,059 --> 00:07:02,069
J. Copeland, ran to the scene.

128
00:07:02,299 --> 00:07:05,939
They waded straight into the molasses and
began pulling people out.

129
00:07:05,979 --> 00:07:07,379
Boston police arrived.

130
00:07:07,419 --> 00:07:10,629
So did the Red Cross, and men from the
Army and the Navy.

131
00:07:10,859 --> 00:07:13,589
Red Cross nurses waded into the molasses
as well.

132
00:07:13,739 --> 00:07:15,989
A makeshift hospital was set up nearby.

133
00:07:16,219 --> 00:07:20,619
One of the most common problems doctors
saw was coughing: people had breathed in

134
00:07:20,659 --> 00:07:24,259
the molasses. The search went on for four
days.

135
00:07:24,299 --> 00:07:28,389
Many of the dead were so covered in
molasses that they were hard to identify.

136
00:07:28,459 --> 00:07:30,739
Some victims had been swept into the
harbor.

137
00:07:30,779 --> 00:07:33,349
They were not found for three to four
months.

138
00:07:33,659 --> 00:07:35,379
Twenty-one people died.

139
00:07:35,419 --> 00:07:39,459
They were workers, drivers, firefighters,
neighbors.

140
00:07:39,499 --> 00:07:44,739
The youngest were two ten-year-olds: Maria
Di Stasio and Pasquale Iantosca.

141
00:07:44,779 --> 00:07:47,779
Among them was a firefighter, George
Layhe.

142
00:07:47,819 --> 00:07:49,589
Several horses died too.

143
00:07:49,739 --> 00:07:52,549
And about a hundred and fifty people were
injured.

144
00:07:56,062 --> 00:07:58,942
It's easy to lose people inside numbers.

145
00:07:58,982 --> 00:08:00,342
So here are some of them.

146
00:08:00,382 --> 00:08:02,582
Patrick Breen, forty-four.

147
00:08:02,622 --> 00:08:04,622
William Brogan, sixty-one.

148
00:08:04,662 --> 00:08:06,902
Bridget Clougherty, sixty-five.

149
00:08:06,942 --> 00:08:09,262
And Stephen Clougherty, thirty-four.

150
00:08:09,302 --> 00:08:11,702
Flaminio Gallerani, thirty-seven.

151
00:08:11,742 --> 00:08:13,822
Eric Laird, seventeen.

152
00:08:13,862 --> 00:08:15,822
Peter Shaughnessy, eighteen.

153
00:08:15,862 --> 00:08:17,942
Ralph Martin, twenty-one.

154
00:08:17,982 --> 00:08:20,102
Michael Sinnott, seventy-eight.

155
00:08:20,142 --> 00:08:22,102
Maria Di Stasio, ten.

156
00:08:22,142 --> 00:08:24,622
Pasquale Iantosca, ten.

157
00:08:24,662 --> 00:08:26,542
Some were working near the tank.

158
00:08:26,582 --> 00:08:28,062
Some lived beside it.

159
00:08:28,102 --> 00:08:30,582
Some were simply walking by at lunchtime.

160
00:08:30,622 --> 00:08:33,832
None of them had any say in how that tank
was built.

161
00:08:37,347 --> 00:08:38,987
Then came the cleanup.

162
00:08:39,027 --> 00:08:43,187
Workers sprayed the streets with salt
water from a fireboat, and spread sand to

163
00:08:43,227 --> 00:08:44,877
soak up the molasses.

164
00:08:44,947 --> 00:08:48,557
Several hundred people worked for weeks to
clear the area.

165
00:08:48,627 --> 00:08:50,877
The molasses didn't stay in the North End.

166
00:08:50,947 --> 00:08:54,187
It was carried across the city on shoes
and clothes.

167
00:08:54,227 --> 00:08:59,437
It turned up on subway platforms, on
streetcar seats, on telephones, in homes.

168
00:08:59,667 --> 00:09:04,627
As one account put it: "Everything that a
Bostonian touched was sticky." The water

169
00:09:04,667 --> 00:09:07,757
of Boston Harbor stayed brown until the
summer.

170
00:09:11,267 --> 00:09:14,557
Now the question was: who was responsible?

171
00:09:14,627 --> 00:09:16,717
USIA had an answer ready.

172
00:09:16,867 --> 00:09:19,277
The tank, it said, had been blown up.

173
00:09:19,427 --> 00:09:22,797
By anarchists. It was not a random claim.

174
00:09:23,027 --> 00:09:26,627
Some of the alcohol made from that
molasses was meant for munitions, and in

175
00:09:26,667 --> 00:09:30,187
1919, fear of political bombings was real.

176
00:09:30,227 --> 00:09:33,437
If the tank had been attacked, the company
would not be to blame.

177
00:09:33,667 --> 00:09:35,947
The families did not accept that.

178
00:09:35,987 --> 00:09:40,187
One hundred and nineteen residents took
USIA to court, together.

179
00:09:40,227 --> 00:09:44,147
It became one of the first class-action
lawsuits in Massachusetts.

180
00:09:44,187 --> 00:09:47,867
Today it's seen as a milestone in the
history of corporate regulation.

181
00:09:47,907 --> 00:09:53,347
For the first time, a company like USIA
had to answer, in detail, for how it built

182
00:09:53,387 --> 00:09:54,907
and ran its equipment.

183
00:09:54,947 --> 00:09:58,347
The court appointed an auditor to hear the
case: Hugh W.

184
00:09:58,387 --> 00:10:01,747
Ogden. The hearings lasted three years.

185
00:10:01,787 --> 00:10:06,187
The anarchist theory was tested against
the evidence: the leaks, the warnings, the

186
00:10:06,227 --> 00:10:11,067
steel. In the end, Ogden found that USIA
was responsible.

187
00:10:11,107 --> 00:10:15,067
The company paid six hundred and twenty-
eight thousand dollars in damages.

188
00:10:15,107 --> 00:10:18,907
The families of those who died reportedly
received about seven thousand dollars

189
00:10:18,947 --> 00:10:21,307
each. Not a criminal conviction.

190
00:10:21,347 --> 00:10:22,717
Not a prison sentence.

191
00:10:22,867 --> 00:10:27,387
A bill. But it was an answer to the
question the company had tried to avoid:

192
00:10:27,427 --> 00:10:28,957
this was not an attack.

193
00:10:29,107 --> 00:10:32,077
It was a tank that should never have been
trusted.

194
00:10:35,576 --> 00:10:39,376
Almost a century later, researchers looked
at the tank again.

195
00:10:39,416 --> 00:10:43,976
A 2014 investigation found that the steel
was only about half as thick as it should

196
00:10:44,016 --> 00:10:46,656
have been, even by the standards of the
time.

197
00:10:46,696 --> 00:10:50,056
The steel also lacked manganese, which
made it more brittle.

198
00:10:50,096 --> 00:10:53,376
And the cracks started at badly made rivet
holes.

199
00:10:53,416 --> 00:10:58,896
In 2016, a team at Harvard studied the
flood using 1919 newspapers, maps and

200
00:10:58,936 --> 00:11:03,296
weather reports. They even poured cold
corn syrup over a scale model of the

201
00:11:03,336 --> 00:11:08,336
neighborhood. Their conclusion: the speeds
reported by witnesses were believable.

202
00:11:08,376 --> 00:11:12,866
And the cold made the molasses thicken
quickly, which made rescue so hard.

203
00:11:13,096 --> 00:11:15,536
The disaster also changed the rules.

204
00:11:15,576 --> 00:11:18,176
After the flood, construction laws
changed.

205
00:11:18,216 --> 00:11:21,696
Projects like this now needed to be
overseen by a licensed architect and a

206
00:11:21,736 --> 00:11:24,696
civil engineer. It sounds obvious today.

207
00:11:24,736 --> 00:11:28,856
A structure that holds millions of gallons
should be designed and checked by people

208
00:11:28,896 --> 00:11:34,306
trained to do it. In 1915, on Commercial
Street, nobody had required that.

209
00:11:37,812 --> 00:11:40,382
USIA never rebuilt the tank.

210
00:11:40,612 --> 00:11:42,972
Today, the site is Langone Park.

211
00:11:43,012 --> 00:11:46,622
There's a Little League baseball field, a
playground, and bocce courts.

212
00:11:46,852 --> 00:11:49,582
A small plaque nearby remembers the
disaster.

213
00:11:49,812 --> 00:11:54,812
On the hundredth anniversary, in 2019,
researchers used ground-penetrating radar

214
00:11:54,852 --> 00:11:56,062
under the ballfield.

215
00:11:56,212 --> 00:11:59,982
About twenty inches down, they found the
tank's concrete base.

216
00:12:00,212 --> 00:12:04,412
At a ceremony, the names of all twenty-one
victims were read aloud.

217
00:12:04,452 --> 00:12:09,052
For decades, people in the North End said
that on hot summer days, you could still

218
00:12:09,092 --> 00:12:12,652
smell molasses. The tank leaked for years.

219
00:12:12,692 --> 00:12:15,822
It groaned. People saw it, and heard it.

220
00:12:16,052 --> 00:12:18,812
And the answer was a coat of brown paint.

221
00:12:18,852 --> 00:12:21,902
The Great Molasses Flood was not a freak
of nature.

222
00:12:22,132 --> 00:12:23,492
It was a warning...

223
00:12:23,532 --> 00:12:25,102
that nobody wanted to hear.

224
00:12:25,412 --> 00:12:28,942
Twenty-one people. A hundred and fifty
injured.

225
00:12:29,172 --> 00:12:31,692
A neighborhood buried in molasses.

226
00:12:31,732 --> 00:12:33,262
Not because nobody knew.

227
00:12:33,492 --> 00:12:36,542
Because the people who knew decided it
could wait.

228
00:12:36,852 --> 00:12:41,012
So the next time you hear the Great
Molasses Flood told as a funny story...

229
00:12:41,052 --> 00:12:43,612
remember the streaks on the side of that
tank.

230
00:12:43,652 --> 00:12:45,822
And the brown paint that covered them.
